<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahj al-Balagha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1009</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nahj al-Balagha, 'The Peak of Eloquence', is a collection of sermons, letters and sayings attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and first Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>. It was compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sharif-al-radi">al-Sharif al-Radi</a> and completed around 1009. Admired as a model of Arabic eloquence, it is one of the most important books of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, although the authenticity of parts of it has been debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahj-al-balagha.jpg" alt="Nahj al-Balagha" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad bin Ahmad al-Naqib (محمد بن احمد النقیب) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Folio_from_a_Nahj_al-Balagha.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Nahj al-Balagha, meaning Peak of Eloquence, is a collection of more than two hundred sermons, nearly eighty letters and almost five hundred sayings of Ali, the cousin and son-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, the first Shia Imam and the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> caliph, who reigned from 656 to 661. It is credited to the Shia scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sharif-al-radi">Sharif al-Radi</a>, who died in 1015, more than three hundred years after Ali. Al-Radi was a poet from a distinguished Shia family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> with connections to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, the Shia dynasty that ruled the area. His aims in compiling the book are described as literary, ethical and spiritual, and he gives no chains of transmission for his texts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The sermons and letters offer a commentary on Ali's political career and have served as an ideological basis for Islamic governance. The best known is the letter of instructions to Malik al-Ashtar, Ali's newly appointed governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, who was killed in 657 on the way to his post. It urges justice and compassion for all, regardless of class, creed and colour. Another letter admonishes Uthman ibn Hunayf, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, for attending a lavish banquet, and a letter of life advice is addressed to Ali's eldest son Hasan.</p><p>The book also contains sharp criticism of Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, and disapproval of Talha, Zubayr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, who opposed Ali at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a> in 656, along with passages on morality, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of God and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Nahj al-Balagha is regarded as an example of the most eloquent Arabic and has been the subject of many commentaries, translations and studies by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia authors. The Mu'tazilite scholar Ibn Abil-Hadid, who died in 1258, wrote a comprehensive commentary and called the work below the speech of the Creator but above the speech of creatures. It is commonly kept in Shia households, often beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>.</p><p>Its authenticity has long been debated. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khallikan">Ibn Khallikan</a>, who died in 1282, first suggested that al-Radi or his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sharif-al-murtada">Sharif al-Murtada</a> wrote it rather than compiled it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> theologians Ibn Taymiyyah and al-Dhahabi rejected most of it. The Shia regard it as authentic.</p><p>Scholars such as Husain M. Jafri, Laura Veccia Vaglieri and Muktar Djebli have traced much of its content to earlier works, among them those of al-Ya'qubi, al-Tabari and al-Jahiz. A stylometric study by Sarwar and Mohamed concluded that the book is internally consistent and not the work of al-Radi or his brother. The philosopher Muhammad Husayn Tabatabai answered that for Shia readers the author is Ali.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nahj al-balagha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under their rule Twelver Shia scholarship flourished in Baghdad, with al-Shaykh al-Mufid, al-Sharif al-Murtada and al-Sharif al-Radi, compiler of the <b>Nahj al-Balagha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
